[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Integrate the EST client (/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-client)\n\n\n\nThis guide is for those implementing the device client. The administrator must have prepared the [DMS policy](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/dms). For a complete first run, follow [Enroll and connect your first device](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/quickstarts/enroll-device).\n\nBefore you begin [#before-you-begin]\n\nYou need the public HTTPS DMS URL, server trust obtained through an approved channel, the operation's authentication mode and an admitted identifier. The examples use Bash, OpenSSL 3.x and curl with `--fail-with-body`; use a private working directory and compatible versions of these tools.\n\nPrepare `est-trust.pem` to verify the EST server, `bootstrap.crt` and `bootstrap.key` for initial certificate authentication, and `device-root.pem` as the approved root of the device issuer. The two trust files can contain different roots. For another authentication mode, adapt the credentials according to [Bootstrap identity](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/bootstrap-identity).\n\nReplace the host and `REPLACE_DMS_ID` with those of your installation. The URL's APS is the DMS identifier, not its display name.\n\n```bash\nset -euo pipefail\numask 077\nEST_BASE='https://lamassu.example.com/.well-known/est/REPLACE_DMS_ID'\n```\n\nDownload and inspect cacerts [#download-and-inspect-cacerts]\n\nThe default content is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7. It is not a direct PEM file.\n\n```bash\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem \\\n  --dump-header cacerts.headers --output cacerts.b64 \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/cacerts\"\nopenssl base64 -d -in cacerts.b64 -out cacerts.p7b\nopenssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in cacerts.p7b -print_certs -quiet -out cacerts.pem\nopenssl crl2pkcs7 -nocrl -certfile cacerts.pem | openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -noout\n```\n\nCheck fingerprints, issuers and dates against the [trust distribution](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/trust-distribution) policy. The response can contain a selection of CAs and Lamassu's TLS certificate. Do not turn the entire bundle into trusted roots or use it to authenticate its own initial download. HTTP 200 here does not prove that authentication or issuance works.\n\nPrepare the CSR [#prepare-the-csr]\n\nThe example generates a new EC P-256 key and uses `est-device-001`, which must be available if the DMS registers automatically. The `Common Name` becomes the device identifier. Check that the profile permits the selected key and subject.\n\n```bash\ntest ! -e device.key\nopenssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out device.key\nopenssl req -new -sha256 -key device.key -subj '/CN=est-device-001' -out device.csr\nopenssl req -in device.csr -verify -noout\nopenssl req -in device.csr -outform DER -out device.csr.der\nopenssl base64 -A -in device.csr.der -out device.csr.b64\n```\n\nAn implementation using hardware or its own store must produce the CSR through its resident key. `simpleenroll` sends the request and retains the key in the client. Honor Extensions does not authorize every requested attribute: see [Profiles](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles).\n\nRequest the identity [#request-the-identity]\n\nWith initial `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE` authentication, send the Base64-encoded DER CSR using `Content-Type: application/pkcs10`:\n\n```bash\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem --cert bootstrap.crt --key bootstrap.key \\\n  --header 'Content-Type: application/pkcs10' \\\n  --data-binary @device.csr.b64 \\\n  --dump-header enroll.headers --output enroll.b64 \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/simpleenroll\"\nopenssl base64 -d -in enroll.b64 -out enroll.p7b\nopenssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in enroll.p7b -print_certs -quiet -out device.crt\n```\n\nStop if curl fails. Retain the HTTP code, headers and body for diagnosis; an error response is not decoded as a certificate. The Bash example stops before that conversion. A timeout leaves the operation's result to be checked in Lamassu: do not automatically repeat issuance.\n\nCheck and install the result [#check-and-install-the-result]\n\nFor a root that issues directly:\n\n```bash\nopenssl verify -CAfile device-root.pem -purpose sslclient device.crt\nopenssl x509 -in device.crt -noout -subject -issuer -serial -dates -text\nopenssl x509 -in device.crt -pubkey -noout > certificate-public.pem\nopenssl pkey -pubin -in certificate-public.pem -outform DER -out certificate-public.der\nopenssl pkey -in device.key -pubout -outform DER -out key-public.der\ncmp certificate-public.der key-public.der\n```\n\nAlso verify that the CN is correct, the certificate is a leaf, its uses permit your application and its dates satisfy the profile. `cmp` must exit with code 0. With intermediates, provide the chain through `-untrusted` as described in [PKI validation](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation).\n\nQuery the device: the owning DMS, current serial number and history must match the result. Protect the key/certificate pair and install both using the device's mechanism. Association in Lamassu does not install files in the client. Check a real connection before considering the process complete.\n\nNext steps [#next-steps]\n\nImplement [renewal and recovery](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/renewal-and-recovery) and trust updates. The [EST reference](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-reference) covers endpoints, formats and ServerKeyGen; [troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting) helps distinguish transport, admission and persistence failures.\n"]
